word_count.cpp
```
#include <map>
#include <string>

namespace word_count {

    std::map<std::string, int> words(const std::string& phrase) {
        (void)phrase;
        return {};
    }

}

```

Ok, I will:

1. I scan the phrase one character at a time, appending each letter or digit to a buffer and lowercasing it as I go.
2. When I meet any other character, I flush the buffer by incrementing its entry in a map and clearing it, so punctuation, whitespace, apostrophes, and hyphens all act as separators.
3. After the loop I flush whatever is still in the buffer, because a phrase may end without a trailing separator.
4. Empty phrases and punctuation-only input never fill the buffer, so the returned map stays empty, and the map gives one lowercase key per distinct word with its count.

Here are the diffs for those changes:

