3. For primes congruent to 3 modulo 4, I compute one root with a single modular exponentiation and take the smaller of the two roots.
4. For other odd primes, I use Tonelli-Shanks, tracking the even part of p minus one and updating the root and correction factor until the correction factor becomes one.
