Ok, I will:

I store the elements in a fixed-size std::vector and track a head index plus a current size so the logical order stays separate from the physical layout. On write I place the new element at the tail, computed as head plus size modulo capacity, and I throw std::domain_error when the buffer is already full. On read I throw std::domain_error when the buffer is empty; otherwise I return the element at head and advance head modulo capacity. Overwrite checks fullness: if full it replaces the oldest element and then advances head, otherwise it simply delegates to write. Clear resets head and size to zero while leaving the underlying storage allocated.

