I decide the answer equals binomial(n,k) times the derangement D(n-k), because choosing which k positions stay fixed is independent of arranging the remaining elements with no fixed points.
I compute D(m) iteratively with the recurrence D(0)=1, D(1)=0, D(m)=(m-1)*(D(m-1)+D(m-2)), keeping only the last two values.
I compute the binomial coefficient via the multiplicative formula, using the smaller of k and n-k so intermediate products stay small.
I throw std::invalid_argument when n or k falls outside the range 0 to 20, and return 0 when k is greater than n before doing any arithmetic.
I rely on the n at most 20 bound to guarantee D(20) and every intermediate product fit within unsigned long long.

