2. For each run I write the rune, prefixing it with a backslash when it is a backslash or an ASCII digit, then append the decimal length only when the run is at least two long.
3. For decoding I walk the encoded string, treating a backslash as an escape that makes the next rune literal and returning an error when the input ends right after one.
4. After each character I greedily consume trailing ASCII digits as the repeat count, defaulting to one, and I reject a count of zero or a multi-digit count with a leading zero.
5. Everything works on runes so multibyte characters form single runs, which keeps the round trip lossless.

