Ok, I will:

1. I will represent the graph with a null-prototype object whose keys are vertex names and whose values are arrays of outgoing neighbors.
2. Adding a vertex is idempotent and returns whether it was new; adding an edge first creates any missing endpoints, then avoids duplicate neighbor entries and returns whether the edge was new.
3. Lookups report missing vertices or edges safely, and neighbors returns a copied array so callers cannot mutate the stored adjacency list.
4. Removing an edge updates only the source list, while removing a vertex deletes its own list and scans every remaining list to remove incoming references.
5. The vertices method lists current keys, and self-edges follow the same single-entry rules.

Here are the diffs for those changes:

```diff
--- graph_adjacency_map.js
+++ graph_adjacency_map.js
@@ ... @@
-class Graph {
-  constructor() {}
-
-  addVertex(vertex) {}
-
-  hasVertex(vertex) {}
-
-  addEdge(from, to) {}
-
-  hasEdge(from, to) {}
-
-  neighbors(vertex) {}
-
-  removeEdge(from, to) {}
-
-  removeVertex(vertex) {}
-
-  vertices() {}
-}
