I store accounts in a hash map keyed by an incrementing counter, so ids start at one and are never reused after an account closes. Each account tracks a balance and an overdraft limit as integer cents, which keeps every money calculation exact. Deposits, withdrawals, and transfers validate the amount first, then account existence, then available funds, and a transfer checks every precondition before touching either balance so a rejected transfer leaves both accounts unchanged. Interest walks the accounts, skips non-positive balances, computes balance times the rate divided by ten thousand with truncation, and sums what it credited. Closing pays out a non-negative balance and refuses accounts that still owe money, after which the id disappears for good.

